aterial so some kind of  higher-level concept and then we also  have a day dedicated to problem solving  so where students can work together or  individually and ask us questions and  see what's going on so this is the big  idea of the structure of our course that  it fits into and I'm going to turn this  off for a second so we can pass it over  to Josh and you can talk a little bit  more about how Mathematica comes into  play with this okay so now I want to  talk a little bit about our primary  goals for course design so how do we  want to design this course and what are  we trying to accomplish so we had a few  different goals in mind so are really  our first one was to make sure we had  really clear student expectations what  were we expecting of our students what  were they going to do we really wanted  to make that crystal clear from day one  and continue to our mind and throughout  the semester and our second goal is  really to make really nice manageable  notes and that's kind of vague so I want  to talk about what I mean by that okay  so so there's really two primary ideas  so one is we want these goals to these  excuse me these notes to be manageable  for our students so this means they  shouldn't be too too long or too complex  right remember this is where our  learning starts our students haven't  seen the material yet when they're  reading these notes so they need to be  very basic and easy to digest another we  wanted to make sure that it was a  complete core of material so what I mean  by this is that it it covers the basics  and doesn't branch out too much but the  notes stand alone so later sections in  the notes don't rely on extra things  we've covered earlier only the notes  themselves okay so its standalone and a  third goal which kind of goes along with  the  first and second is we really wanted  this to be easier for new instructors to  pick up the notes and to use them and to  adapt them to their own classes so they  could create their own auxiliary  materials to go with the notes third as  this is kind of the primary point of the  talk is we wanted to engage their  students and higher thinking during the  class this is just a general goal and  kind of going along with the third goal  we wanted to focus on conceptual  understanding instead of computation  we're doing multivariate calculus and as  many of you may be aware if you don't do  this there is an awful lot of  computation that you can have to wade  through in calculus 3 so how did we want  to focus on conceptual understanding  first we really wanted to steep it in a  geometric viewpoint its multivariate  calculus so there's lots and lots of  pictures we wanted to make sure students  could see these and maybe even create  their own pictures on their own so they  can have that viewpoint that everything  is geometric and we wanted to focus on  some main ideas one was linearization  limits additivity and then lastly just  coordinates and different ways of  viewing the different geometries when  you're describing shapes all right so  now I want to talk about how we're going  to accomplish these goals and what  Mathematica has to do with that so how  do we use Mathematica to accomplish all  those goals so just a little bit to say  about the notes again it's a core  material pretty much we just define what  our concepts are and we try to give an  intuitive explanation there's not a lot  of proof theorem and proof and the notes  this is because i feel that theorems are  harder for the students to understand at  this point so it's mostly intuitive  explanations of why things work the way  they do and there's lots and lots of  examples in the notes we actually want  to add even more and these are often  presented through Mathematica so that we  don't really  over much of the computation and the  background we just give them an idea of  how do you approach this problem  conceptually and this is the code that  goes through those steps and the  mathematica is in the notes they can  easily work with it on their own okay so  here's a nice little sample from the  notes so what have we got here so here's  their parameterizing ellipsoid you can  see I'm mostly doing this in tech and  explaining but then I have a nice little  quip down here where I have a bunch of  mathematica code that not only computes  the the ellipse and the tangent plane  but also gives us a nice picture so  that's something that's very common in  our notes just have pictures of all the  different things that are going on and  of course the code so that they can  produce it on their own later okay all  right so let's well let's let's do this  okay so after our students has looked at  notes they do something called pre-class  practice the idea behind this is it's  fine and good to read through notes but  you need some time as a student to work  with the ideas on your own before your  has to do something more complicated and  so this is just a little notebook file  with some introductory questions and  problems and a little bit of guidance  tells the students get started on  everything so and we we present our  commands and bold and we try to  introduce them to the mathematica  commands as we go our students have no  background in Mathematica when they  enter this class so it's really really  important for us that we guide them  step-by-step so they get used to using  the programming okay so finally this is  the in-class part after we have done  notes and practice they come to class  and they do an activity so these  activities are probably the most  elaborate part of our course design and  students show up to class in a computer  lab and they work together in small  groups usually three or four students in  a group and they do a bunch of stuff and  a notebook file so notebook file  typically has a little introduction  in and then some stuff for them to work  through and these are these are very  important these involve Mathematica  because we asked them to do a lot of  computation in just one class period and  it's not really doable without  Mathematica so here's a nice example  where we have them look at limits along  different paths and plot it and see that  it's not continuous at the origin and  then they can prove it by looking at  excuse me by looking at several paths so  you can say they get several answers  okay here's a different one where we're  looking at more of an application vector  fields we had them look at electric  fields plot some of them and we also had  them look at divergence and curl and  discovering some of how the math they've  learned relates to physics and different  applications and the last little example  this one is is probably my favorite here  this is really asking them to dig deep  and a concept that's I think is very  challenging for our students which is  the second derivative test our students  are not required to have linear algebra  before they take count three and most of  them don't know very much linear algebra  so dealing with the second derivative  test and finding I ghen values for for a  hessian matrix doesn't mean much to them  they don't know what an eigenvalue is  and so this activity we tried to let  them visualize that so they looked at  the linearization of the gradient which  uses the Hessian and they can see what  the eigenvalues means something in this  vector field might have to do with  directions of going in and out so so  being able to visualize that allows them  to understand something that normally  they don't have the tools to do just  really cool and so the last part of what  we're doing after they've done  activities and kind of built them up  made them ready we gave them work on  homework on their own and then finally  at the end of the unit they take an exam  and then all of their homework and all  of their exams is done in math  echo and they're allowed to use any  command they like and this makes  computation pretty simple because  they've got a lot of power here with  Mathematica and so to kind of make sure  that there's a check on here they're not  just coding things in that they don't  understand actually most of the grade is  from explaining their answers so they  have to explain in text in words what's  what's going on how does their solution  work and why does it work and here's a  nice little quip from an exam of a  student solution so we asked them to  find a distance between a circle and a  line and you can see they have a little  list of how they're doing it and then  why that method works you can see the  code is actually a very small part of  their answer here not too too much okay  and they have a little bottom line okay  so this is our first semester  implementing these notes in these tools  but based on mid semester feedback we  already have some changes we want to put  in place for even our next unit and next  semester I looking to the beginning of  next semester we want to create a  smoother start to the course our  students have never seen or used  Mathematica before for the most part so  throwing calculus 3 and mathematica at  them all in that first couple weeks can  be a little bit overwhelming so we want  to try to figure out how to kind of ease  them in to using Mathematica it's nice  at the beginning they can check their  work by hand because we're doing vector  calculus at the beginning so it's  something that's easy to compute and  double check yourself by hand but  eventually once we get further into the  material mathematic is going to be the  main thing if you checked yourself by  hand it would take a very very long time  so in an effort to create a smoother  start we want to add more Mathematica  based explanations to the first couple  units and also to transition the  pre-class to be more of a guided  practice so that we can introduce them  maybe more fluidly to command syntax  that kind of thing so that's something  I'm actually hoping to put into place  starting next week  to create kind of this guided practice  feel instead of a list of problems and a  to-do list also something we're looking  forward to tackling for next in part  next semester is to create some videos  to go along with our notes right now we  have all the kind of written material a  handle on that but creating videos so  that we can appeal to more varieties of  learning and kind of give them options  they didn't understand understand  something when they read it may be  watching it will help or kind of playing  around with it that way so it was that's  what we're looking forward to do next  semester and next week so thank you for  listening do you guys have any questions
 

